// ============================================================
// DAX measures for the Retail Sales Dashboard
// Table name used below: 'Orders' (rename your table to Orders
// in Power BI, or replace 'Orders' with your table's name).
// The revenue column is called: revenue
// The date column is called:   order_date
//
// HOW TO ADD A MEASURE (same steps every time):
//   1. In the Fields pane (right side), right-click the Orders table.
//   2. Click "New measure".
//   3. Delete what is in the formula bar, type ONE measure below
//      (the part after the // comments), and press Enter.
//   4. Repeat for each measure. They will show up in the Fields
//      pane with a small calculator icon.
// ============================================================


// ---------- 1. Total Revenue ----------
// Adds up the revenue column. Because it is a measure (not a
// column), it reacts to slicers: filter to "Clothing" and this
// number becomes Clothing revenue only.

Total Revenue = SUM ( Orders[revenue] )


// ---------- 2. Total Orders ----------
// Counts the order rows. Every row in the data is one order,
// so a simple row count works.

Total Orders = COUNTROWS ( Orders )


// ---------- 3. Average Order Value ----------
// Total money divided by number of orders.
// DIVIDE is used instead of "/" because DIVIDE safely returns
// blank instead of an error if orders are ever zero.

Average Order Value = DIVIDE ( [Total Revenue], [Total Orders] )


// ---------- 4. Category Revenue Share (%) ----------
// This one is used INSIDE the category bar chart.
// CALCULATE + ALL removes the category filter for the
// denominator only, so each bar is divided by the grand total.
// Example: Clothing shows about 31.2%.

Category Share % =
DIVIDE (
    [Total Revenue],
    CALCULATE ( [Total Revenue], ALL ( Orders[category] ) )
)


// ---------- 5. Month-over-Month Revenue Change (%) ----------
// Compares this month's revenue with last month's.
// DATEADD shifts the dates back 1 month to get "last month".
// The monthly line chart needs the MonthStart column described
// at the bottom of this file for this measure to work.
// Example: November 2024 shows +73.2% (it jumped to $14,948.60).

Revenue Last Month =
CALCULATE ( [Total Revenue], DATEADD ( Orders[MonthStart], -1, MONTH ) )

MoM Revenue Change % =
DIVIDE ( [Total Revenue] - [Revenue Last Month], [Revenue Last Month] )


// ---------- 6. Best Month Revenue ----------
// The highest single month of revenue in the data.
// In this dashboard it returns $14,948.60 (November 2024).
// SUMMARIZE groups revenue by month first, then MAXX picks
// the largest monthly value.

Best Month Revenue =
MAXX (
    SUMMARIZE ( Orders, Orders[MonthStart], "MonthlyRevenue", [Total Revenue] ),
    [MonthlyRevenue]
)


// ============================================================
// DATE COLUMNS AND THE DATE TABLE NOTE
// ============================================================
// This dashboard only covers one year (Jan-Dec 2024), so a
// simple helper column is enough instead of a full date table:
//
//   1. In the Fields pane, right-click Orders -> "New column".
//   2. Name it MonthStart and use:
//        MonthStart = DATE ( YEAR ( Orders[order_date] ), MONTH ( Orders[order_date] ), 1 )
//      This turns every order date into the 1st of its month,
//      which is what the line chart axis and the MoM measure use.
//   3. Also make a label column so the chart shows "Jan", "Feb"...:
//        MonthName = FORMAT ( Orders[MonthStart], "mmm" )
//
// NOTE for bigger projects: when data spans more than one year,
// the proper way is a separate calendar ("date") table with one
// row per day, marked as the date table, and related to
// Orders[order_date]. The MoM measure above would then use the
// date table's date column instead of Orders[MonthStart].
// ============================================================
